WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.950
I asked an agent to

2
00:00:01.950 --> 00:00:04.210
explain the term malware.

3
00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:06.900
It looked up “the term

4
00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:10.580
malware”, not “malware”!

5
00:00:11.060 --> 00:00:14.240
So how does a model choose a

6
00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:16.660
tool, and why does it slip?

7
00:00:17.970 --> 00:00:20.650
Today: how an agent picks a tool,

8
00:00:21.150 --> 00:00:22.250
and fills in the details.

9
00:00:23.690 --> 00:00:25.630
Before your request, the app

10
00:00:25.630 --> 00:00:27.930
writes a tools line: the names

11
00:00:27.930 --> 00:00:29.070
of the tools that are on.

12
00:00:29.650 --> 00:00:31.710
The model reads that line and

13
00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:33.790
your words, and nothing else.

14
00:00:34.490 --> 00:00:37.850
From them, it picks one tool, or none.

15
00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:40.680
Each tool needs details,

16
00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:42.800
like a place and a day.

17
00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:45.910
The model copies them from your words

18
00:00:45.910 --> 00:00:48.630
into the call, so Kafi stays Kafi.

19
00:00:49.190 --> 00:00:51.390
Days and times go in a fixed

20
00:00:51.390 --> 00:00:52.950
form that the tool can read.

21
00:00:54.190 --> 00:00:55.750
The details in a call are

22
00:00:55.750 --> 00:00:56.830
called its arguments.

23
00:00:57.680 --> 00:00:59.960
Each tool has its own: the weather

24
00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:02.440
needs a place and a day, a reminder

25
00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:04.160
needs its text and a time.

26
00:01:04.840 --> 00:01:07.620
When one is missing, a good agent asks.

27
00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:11.000
How good is Darsi Tiny?

28
00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:13.900
With wordings it was trained on, it gets

29
00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:15.980
the tool and every argument right

30
00:01:15.980 --> 00:01:18.360
ninety-six to ninety-eight times in a

31
00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:18.560
hundred.

32
00:01:19.180 --> 00:01:20.820
With new wordings, about

33
00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:22.200
seventy-eight to eighty.

34
00:01:22.870 --> 00:01:25.350
That’s roughly one slip in five.

35
00:01:26.690 --> 00:01:27.170
Quick check!

36
00:01:27.810 --> 00:01:30.290
Why does a new way of asking make

37
00:01:30.290 --> 00:01:32.370
Darsi Tiny slip more often?

38
00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:35.260
It learned patterns of wording.

39
00:01:36.010 --> 00:01:38.350
A small model has seen each kind of

40
00:01:38.350 --> 00:01:41.230
request in a few dozen forms, and a

41
00:01:41.230 --> 00:01:43.090
new form fits them less well.

42
00:01:43.700 --> 00:01:46.680
Big models have seen far more, so they

43
00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:49.200
slip less, but they still slip.

44
00:01:50.420 --> 00:01:52.420
Here are real slips from the lab.

45
00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:55.960
Asked about class 9Z, which doesn’t

46
00:01:55.960 --> 00:01:59.280
exist, it wrote 9C, a class it knows.

47
00:02:00.030 --> 00:02:02.350
And the capital of France, asked

48
00:02:02.350 --> 00:02:04.650
one way, sent it to the glossary

49
00:02:04.650 --> 00:02:06.410
instead of saying it can’t.

50
00:02:07.490 --> 00:02:09.490
So the app checks every

51
00:02:09.490 --> 00:02:10.630
call before it runs.

52
00:02:11.350 --> 00:02:12.290
Is the tool on?

53
00:02:12.950 --> 00:02:14.950
Did each name come from your words?

54
00:02:15.730 --> 00:02:17.830
Is the time a real time?

55
00:02:18.530 --> 00:02:20.690
A few lines of code catch these slips.

56
00:02:22.030 --> 00:02:23.710
In the app, switch the weather

57
00:02:23.710 --> 00:02:25.670
tool off and ask about it.

58
00:02:26.140 --> 00:02:29.220
Ask what 9Z has tomorrow, and read

59
00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:30.260
the check under the call.

60
00:02:30.830 --> 00:02:33.670
Then ask about France, in two ways.

61
00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:35.540
Your turn.

62
00:02:36.150 --> 00:02:38.550
I asked for a reminder at eight,

63
00:02:39.170 --> 00:02:40.450
and the call says nine.

64
00:02:41.210 --> 00:02:42.330
What should the app do?

65
00:02:43.610 --> 00:02:45.450
Stop it, and ask me again:

66
00:02:46.110 --> 00:02:47.670
nine isn’t what I said.

67
00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:50.380
Remember: the model reads the

68
00:02:50.380 --> 00:02:52.420
tools line and your words.

69
00:02:53.170 --> 00:02:55.150
It copies details into arguments,

70
00:02:55.650 --> 00:02:57.570
and new wordings slip more.

71
00:02:58.210 --> 00:03:00.190
So the app checks every call.

72
00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:03.320
Try it in class tomorrow: one

73
00:03:03.320 --> 00:03:05.340
pupil plays the agent, with a

74
00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:06.560
card of request patterns.

75
00:03:07.270 --> 00:03:08.890
Classmates ask for the same

76
00:03:08.890 --> 00:03:10.330
thing in five ways.

77
00:03:11.020 --> 00:03:13.440
Which wordings make the agent slip?
